## Memoisation and the React Compiler

The React Compiler (on in Expo SDK 54+ templates, `experiments.reactCompiler` in
`app.json`) memoizes components, callbacks and derived values automatically.

- Do **not** ask for `React.memo`, `useCallback` or `useMemo` as a default. Flag
  hand-written ones that have no comment naming the measured problem (SUGGESTION).
- A manual memo is justified for a measured problem (React DevTools profiler, dropped
  frames, FlashList blank cells) or an identity a non-React API depends on.
- Flag code the compiler skips because it breaks the Rules of React (WARNING): mutating
  props, state or captured values during render, reading `ref.current` in render,
  conditional hooks. `npx react-compiler-healthcheck` or the `react-hooks` ESLint
  rules point at those components.
- React 19: `ref` is a normal prop; new `forwardRef` wrappers are a SUGGESTION to remove.

```typescript
// CORRECT - plain code
export function ItemCard({ item, onPress }: ItemCardProps) {
  return (
    <Pressable onPress={() => onPress?.(item.id)}>
      <Text>{item.name}</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
}
const filteredItems = filterCategory
  ? items.filter((item) => item.category === filterCategory)
  : items;

// WRONG - breaks the Rules of React, so the compiler bails out
function ItemCard({ item }: ItemCardProps) {
  item.viewed = true; // mutating a prop during render
  return <Text>{item.name}</Text>;
}
```

